<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>I presented you with an elaborated descriptio=
n of this diagram, essentially, which I spent quite a bit of time formulati=
ng=E2=80=94probably about 25 years ago, I guess, which kind of accounts for=
 its graphic primitiveness. I was really pushing the limits of my 486 compu=
ter to produce that, I can tell you. It=E2=80=99s a representation of the a=
rchetypal circumstances of life. The archetypal circumstances are the circu=
mstances that are true under all conditions for all time. So you can think =
about them as descriptively characteristic of the nature of human experienc=
e. That=E2=80=99s not exactly the same as the nature of reality, because yo=
u can divide reality into its subjective and objective elements. There=E2=
=80=99s utility in doing that. But these sorts of representations don=E2=80=
=99t play that game. They consider human experience as constitutive of real=
ity. That=E2=80=99s how we experience it, and so we=E2=80=99ll just go with=
 that.=20
<br><br>The idea, basically, is that we always exist inside a damaged struc=
ture. That structure is partly biological and partly sociocultural. It=E2=
=80=99s partly what=E2=80=99s been handed to us by our ancestors, both prac=
tically, in terms of infrastructure, and psychologically, in terms of the a=
ctive, learned content of our psyches. That would include, for example, our=
 ability to utilize language, the words that we use, the phrases that we us=
e, and the mutual understanding that we developed as a consequence of inter=
acting with each other. Archetypally speaking, that structure=E2=80=99s alw=
ays dead and corrupt. The reason it=E2=80=99s dead is because it was made b=
y people who are dead. The reason it=E2=80=99s corrupt is because things fa=
ll apart of their own accord. The fact that people don=E2=80=99t aim proper=
ly, let=E2=80=99s say, speeds along that process of degeneration. What that=
 means is that young people always have a reason to be upset and cynical ab=
out the current state of affairs. It=E2=80=99s forever that way.
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s useful, I think, to consider such conceptualizations a=
s the patriarchy in that light. It=E2=80=99s an archetypal truth that the s=
ocial structure is corrupt and incomplete. What that means is that it=E2=80=
=99s something that you have to contend with in every moment of your life. =
It=E2=80=99s a permanent fact of existence, and to be upset that the social=
 structures=E2=80=94or even the biological structures=E2=80=94within which =
we live are incomplete and imperfect, and to take that personally=E2=80=A6T=
hat=E2=80=99s the worst part of it. To take that personally is to misread t=
he existential condition of humankind.=20
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s always the case that what you have been given, and wha=
t you live in, is degenerate, corrupt, and in need of repair. It=E2=80=99s =
easier to just accept that, because there=E2=80=99s also a positive element=
. The positive element is, well, you=E2=80=99ve been granted something, rat=
her than nothing, and maybe you haven=E2=80=99t been granted pure hell. The=
re=E2=80=99s room for gratitude, especially in a country like ours, where m=
any things actually function quite well. Even if it=E2=80=99s a broken mach=
ine, it=E2=80=99s not one that=E2=80=99s completely devastated, and it=E2=
=80=99s not absolutely hellbent, at every second, on your misery and destru=
ction. It easily could be. Many societies are like that. The fact that we h=
appen to live in one that isn=E2=80=99t corrupt beyond imagining is somethi=
ng to be eternally grateful for.<input class=3D"spoilerbutton" type=3D"butt=
