<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>So we live inside a damaged structure. We als=
o bear responsibility for that damage, because we don=E2=80=99t do everythi=
ng we can to constantly repair it. People say, what=E2=80=99s the meaning o=
f life? What they really mean is, what=E2=80=99s the positive meaning of li=
fe? Because=E2=80=94as we=E2=80=99ve already discussed=E2=80=94the negative=
 meanings of life are more or less self-evident. Well, the positive meaning=
 of life is to be found in noting the state of lack of repair of the walled=
 city that you inhabit, and then sallying forth to do something about that=
=E2=80=94to repair the breaches, fix up the walls, and to make the structur=
e that you inhabit as secure and as productive as it possibly can be.
<br><br>There=E2=80=99s no shortage of opportunities to do that. You can do=
 that in your own mind; you can do that in your own room; you can do that i=
n your own household; you can do that in your own local community. Maybe, i=
f you get good at doing that at all those levels, you can start to look bey=
ond that. There=E2=80=99s challenges. That=E2=80=99s the thing that=E2=80=
=99s kind of interesting about this insufficient structure: it has a set of=
 challenges built into it, because of its insufficiency, and, perhaps, even=
 because of its corrupt nature. It calls forth the potential response from =
you of heroic adventure. Heroic adventure is to man the barricades and repa=
ir the city. You can always do that. It doesn=E2=80=99t matter what your pe=
rsonal circumstances are. There=E2=80=99s always something that isn=E2=80=
=99t right near you=E2=80=94that isn=E2=80=99t correct or laid out properly=
=E2=80=94that you could just fix, if you wanted to.
<br><br>One of the things that we=E2=80=99re going to talk about tonight is=
 the idea that, if you adopt an attitude that=E2=80=99s like that, the rule=
 that you should play is to make things better, wherever you are, however y=
ou can. What would actually happen would be that things would get better, w=
herever you are, in all sorts of ways. We=E2=80=99ve really, as a species, =
you might say=E2=80=94or even as singular individuals=E2=80=94explored that=
 rarely. It isn=E2=80=99t something that=E2=80=99s put forth as a propositi=
on that often. It=E2=80=99s quite surprising to me.=20
<br><br>I had an interesting experience the other day. I went to The Keg. I=
 go there because I have food allergies, and they=E2=80=99re very careful w=
ith people who have food allergies. The waiter took me to the table. He sai=
d that he=E2=80=99d been watching my lectures. That=E2=80=99s a very common=
 experience. He said that he=E2=80=99d had two promotions at The Keg in the=
 past four months because he=E2=80=99d been watching my lectures. I really =
found that an affecting experience. You might say, well, he=E2=80=99s worki=
ng as a waiter at The Keg, and there=E2=80=99s nothing particularly heroic =
about that. I disagree with that, actually, because I don=E2=80=99t care wh=
ere you=E2=80=99re located.=20
<br><br>You can do a hell of a job. I mean that literally. You can take wha=
tever job you have and make it a real nice little piece of absolute misery.=
 Or you can act like a civilized human being and notice that, no matter whe=
re you are, there=E2=80=99s a richness and a complexity that=E2=80=99s comp=
letely inexhaustible, right at hand. And then you can take that seriously. =
You can say, well, I happen to be a waiter at The Keg. Perhaps that=E2=80=
=99s not what I expected=E2=80=94and he=E2=80=99s a young guy=E2=80=94and p=
erhaps that=E2=80=99s not where I want to end up, but it=E2=80=99s not noth=
ing. It=E2=80=99s a rich environment, and I can make it a lot better, if I =
want to. I can get along properly with my coworkers, and not gossip behind =
their back. I can treat my customers properly. And if an opportunity comes =
my way, I can take it, and I can see what happens.
<br><br>He said that=E2=80=99s what he started doing. Things were working o=
ut much better for him. He was in a much better job than he was three month=
s ago. In three months; that=E2=80=99s nothing. That=E2=80=99s a nice traje=
ctory. It=E2=80=99s an uphill trajectory. That=E2=80=99s what you want, rea=
lly. An uphill trajectory=E2=80=99s actually better than being somewhere go=
od, as far as I=E2=80=99m concerned. One of the things that really makes yo=
ur life meaningful is the clear realization that you=E2=80=99re heading som=
ewhere better than where you are now. And then it=E2=80=99s even better if =
you also understand that there=E2=80=99s a direct causal relationship betwe=
en the things that you=E2=80=99re doing and the steepness of that incline.
<br><br>I get a lot of letters from people like that. They are most frequen=
tly young men, although not always. They say, I=E2=80=99ve been listening t=
o these lectures, and I=E2=80=99ve decided that I=E2=80=99m going to try to=
 take responsibility for my life. I started to stop doing all the stupid th=
ings that I know are stupid and that I shouldn=E2=80=99t be doing. I=E2=80=
=99ve started doing some of the things that aren=E2=80=99t stupid that I kn=
ow I should be doing. It seems pretty obvious, really, if you think about i=
t. But, obvious though it may be, that isn=E2=80=99t necessarily what peopl=
e do. And then they write and say, you can=E2=80=99t believe what differenc=
e that makes. They=E2=80=99re thrilled about it, and so I=E2=80=99m thrille=
d about it.=20
<br><br>I really don=E2=80=99t experience anything that=E2=80=99s better th=
an a letter like that or a message like that. It=E2=80=99s so good to see t=
hings that aren=E2=80=99t so good replaced by something better. I truly bel=
ieve it=E2=80=99s an open question: to what degree could we make things bet=
ter if that=E2=80=99s what we actually aimed at doing? In some of the stori=
es that we=E2=80=99ve covered already=E2=80=94the story of Cain and Abel, i=
n particular=E2=80=94there is really an analysis of that problem, which is =
so remarkable. It occurs so early in this document. It=E2=80=99s such a fou=
ndational story. It basically says that there=E2=80=99s two modes of being =
in the world: there=E2=80=99s one where you adopt the responsibility for be=
ing properly, and you make the sacrifices necessary for doing that. Then ev=
erything will flourish properly. The other one is a pathway of resentment, =
bitterness, rejection, murder, and genocide. And that just seems exactly ri=
ght to me.=20
<br><br>Carl Jung once said that modern people didn=E2=80=99t see God becau=
se they didn=E2=80=99t look low enough. That=E2=80=99s a phrase that I real=
ly, really like. People denigrate the opportunities that are right in front=
 of them. There=E2=80=99s no reason to do that. What=E2=80=99s right in fro=
nt of you is the majesty of being. That=E2=80=99s what=E2=80=99s right in f=
ront of you. It=E2=80=99s inexhaustibly complex and full of potential. Ther=
e=E2=80=99s no reason to assume that wherever you happen to be isn=E2=80=99=
t as a good of a starting place as anywhere else. I know some people have t=
errible, terrible lives. They are in situations that are absolutely unbeara=
ble. But I also do know that even situations like that can be made a hell o=
f a lot worse by the worst kind of attitude. That=E2=80=99s for sure.<input=
