<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>That=E2=80=99s where you are. You=E2=80=99re =
in a damaged structure=E2=80=94you are a damaged structure. But at least it=
=E2=80=99s got some walls. You=E2=80=99re not being fed to the lions on a r=
egular basis. That=E2=80=99s a good thing. You can emerge forward heroicall=
y, magically to confront the chaos that constantly threatens the structure =
within which you live. You can free something as a consequence of that. You=
 can learn something; you can strengthen yourself. That=E2=80=99s the other=
 thing: what informs you, and what you=E2=80=99re made of, is what you enco=
unter when you voluntarily encounter the unknown. The more you voluntarily =
encounter the unknown, the more you get made of. The more you get made of, =
the more there is to you. And the more you=E2=80=99re good at encountering =
the unknown, restructuring order, and calling forth proper order out of the=
 potential of being=E2=80=A6God, you got to think, why wouldn=E2=80=99t you=
 do that, since you can do that?=20
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s an endless mystery. It=E2=80=99s also encapsulated, to=
 some degree, in the story of Adam and Eve. What happens to Adam is that he=
 becomes self-conscious and ashamed of himself. He regards himself as a low=
ly sort of creature. There=E2=80=99s endless reasons why people would do th=
at. We=E2=80=99re rife with imperfection, and so Adam hides from God. I thi=
nk that=E2=80=99s actually the answer to the conundrum: people don=E2=80=99=
t aspire to the highest good because they=E2=80=99re deeply ashamed of them=
selves, their weaknesses, and their insufficiencies. That=E2=80=99s not the=
 only reason. There=E2=80=99s the desire to avoid responsibility, and there=
=E2=80=99s all the negative motivations, as well, like resentment, hatred, =
and the desire to make things worse. I don=E2=80=99t want to give us too mu=
ch of a break. It=E2=80=99s something like that. But it=E2=80=99s ok to not=
 be in a very good place if what you=E2=80=99re trying to do with that not =
very good place is to make it better.=20
<br><br>One of the things that I really have learned as a clinical psycholo=
gist is that you just could not believe how powerful incremental progress i=
s. You can do the calculations. It=E2=80=99s like compound interest. If you=
 make your life a tenth of a percent better a week=E2=80=A6Man, in two or t=
hree years, you=E2=80=99re in such a better place than where you were that =
it isn=E2=80=99t even like the same domain. If you keep that up for ten or =
twenty years=E2=80=94especially if you=E2=80=99re young, start to straighte=
n yourself out early, and start to fix the things that you can fix=E2=80=94=
you can transform your lives in ways that are completely unimaginable. God =
only knows what the upper limit of that is in terms of human possibility. W=
e are amazing creatures when we really get our act together and stop runnin=
g at 10 percent of our capacity.
<br><br>So that=E2=80=99s what you do. The fact that things aren=E2=80=99t =
exactly the way that they should be at least gives you something to do, and=
 maybe something great to do, because there=E2=80=99s no shortage of suffer=
ing and trouble that besets the world that you could conceivably ameliorate=
. The utility and intrinsic meaning of that is self-evident. It also makes =
me curious about nihilism, for example, and despair. I understand those emo=
tions. I understand them deeply, and the intellectual mindset that goes alo=
ng with it. But they just seem beside the point, in some sense, because the=
re are so many things that need doing that all you really have to do is ope=
n your eyes, look at them, and then decide that you=E2=80=99re actually goi=
ng to do something about them. You might think, well, what=E2=80=99s within=
 my scope of influence is so trivial that it=E2=80=99s not wroth doing. It=
=E2=80=99s like, it won=E2=80=99t stay trivial for long if you do it. Not a=
t all, and I don=E2=80=99t think it=E2=80=99s trivial to begin with. I real=
ly don=E2=80=99t believe that anything done right is trivial.=20
<br><br>My experience, in my life, has been that anything that I actually d=
id paid off. It didn=E2=80=99t pay off necessarily in the way that I expect=
ed it to pay off. That=E2=80=99s a whole different story. But if it was gen=
uine commitment to do something, even if it went sideways and the outcome w=
as really something other than what I expected, the net consequence, over t=
ime, was nothing but good. Every new frontier that can be conquered is an a=
dvance forward, and there=E2=80=99s no shortage of frontier. We=E2=80=99re =
surrounded by the unknown. We=E2=80=99re surrounded by our own ignorance. W=
e can continually move into the domain of chaos=E2=80=94or we can restructu=
re pathological order. That=E2=80=99s the secret to proper being.=20
<br><br>So then you encounter chaos, and then you can regard yourself as th=
e sort of entity that, despite its insufficiency, has the capability to con=
quer chaos=E2=80=94despite the danger of that. That=E2=80=99s the other thi=
ng. The fact that you=E2=80=99re fragile is actually a precondition for you=
r heroism. If you weren=E2=80=99t fragile, there wouldn=E2=80=99t be anythi=
ng heroic about doing something difficult, right? If you couldn=E2=80=99t b=
e hurt, damaged, defeated, or end up in failure, then where=E2=80=99s the m=
oral courage in the endeavour? It has to be that the fragility is built int=
o the courage. It=E2=80=99s not a reason not to engage in it, at all. In fa=
ct, quite the contrary.<input class=3D"spoilerbutton" type=3D"button" value=
