<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>So what do you do? Well, you put the city bac=
k together, and maybe the way you want it, so it=E2=80=99s functional, effi=
cient, and beautiful, and so that people can flourish in a manner that make=
s them feel that the unbearable catastrophe of being is worth it for the ex=
perience. That=E2=80=99s what you=E2=80=99re aiming at. It=E2=80=99s not an=
 impossibility. And then, not only do you repair the city when you do that,=
 but you make yourself the sort of thing that continually repairs the city.=
 That=E2=80=99s even better. That=E2=80=99s the end goal. It=E2=80=99s not =
the repair of the city that=E2=80=99s the goal: it=E2=80=99s the transforma=
tion of yourself into the thing that continually repairs the city. There=E2=
=80=99s just no reason for that not to happen. The more it can happen, the =
better.
<br><br>There=E2=80=99s an undercurrent to the story of the flood, and that=
=E2=80=99s the fact that the city can become corrupt, and that=E2=80=99s be=
cause people don=E2=80=99t engage in heroic endeavour=E2=80=94or, perhaps, =
because they engage in precisely the opposite of that, which is outright de=
structive behaviour. This is also something that=E2=80=99s worth considerin=
g, too. If you consider your own manner of being, you can say things to peo=
ple, such as, tell the truth and be good. Those are cliches, obviously, and=
 so they lack power. But you can take them apart,and utilize them in a mann=
er that stops being a cliche. You do that by being more humble about them, =
I would say.=20
<br><br>Maybe you can=E2=80=99t tell the truth because you don=E2=80=99t kn=
ow what the truth is. But one thing you can do is to stop saying things tha=
t you know to be untrue. You might say, well, how do I know that they=E2=80=
=99re untrue? Well, you need a whole elaboration of a philosophy of truth t=
o answer that question. We=E2=80=99re not going to bother with that questio=
n, because, at the moment, it=E2=80=99s beside the point. That isn=E2=80=99=
t the issue. The issue is that there are times in your life where you know =
that the thing that you=E2=80=99re saying is not true. It=E2=80=99s a decep=
tion. It=E2=80=99s a lie of some sort, and you=E2=80=99re using it to manip=
ulate yourself, another person, or the world. You=E2=80=99re also fully pos=
sessed by the idea that you can get away with it.=20
<br><br>There=E2=80=99s a Satanic arrogance about that. In fact, that is th=
e archetypal arrogance that=E2=80=99s portrayed in the mythological charact=
er of Satan. Satan is precisely the archetype of the element of the mind th=
at believes it can twist and bend the structure of reality without paying t=
he price. You can=E2=80=99t imagine anything that=E2=80=99s more arrogant t=
han that. You really think that you can twist the structure of reality? And=
 that that=E2=80=99s going to work out for you, without it snapping back? I=
t=E2=80=99s so obvious that that can=E2=80=99t work that everyone knows it.
<br><br>Anyways, back to the initial point. You know, by the rules of the g=
ame that you yourself are playing, that, some of the time, you=E2=80=99re v=
iolating the rules of the game that you=E2=80=99re playing. The first issue=
 with regards to, say, stating the truth or behaving in a responsible manne=
r would be merely to stop cheating at whatever game it is that you=E2=80=99=
ve chosen to play. That=E2=80=99s a good start. That=E2=80=99ll straighten =
out your life.
<br><br><a id=3D"C2"></a><div align=3D"middle"><a id=3D"C2"><span class=3D"=
"><font size=3D"4">Section II</font></span></a><br><font size=3D"1"><a href=
=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://youtu.be/6gFjB9FTN58=
?t=3D1291" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">TIMESTAMP</span></a><=
/font></div>Well, how does the flood tie into this? We live in a corrupt st=
ructure, and we=E2=80=99re corrupt as individuals. Part of that corruption =
is just happenstance. It=E2=80=99s the way things fall apart. But the other=
 part of it is that, not only are we not aiming up, but we=E2=80=99re actua=
lly aiming down. The flood story=E2=80=99s a warning, and it=E2=80=99s a ve=
ry clear warning. The warning is that, if you aim down enough, and then if =
enough of you aim down at the same time, everything will degenerate into so=
mething that=E2=80=99s indistinguishable from the chaos from which things e=
merge at the beginning of time. It=E2=80=99s something like that.=20
<br><br>The cosmos that=E2=80=99s presented in mythological representations=
 is chaos versus order. The order is on top, you might say, and the chaos i=
s always underneath. The chaos can break through, or the order can crumble,=
 and you can fall into the chaos. That chaos is intermingled potential. The=
 way that you destroy the order and let the chaos rise back up=E2=80=94whic=
h is exactly how it=E2=80=99s portrayed in the flood story=E2=80=94is by in=
habiting the corpse of your father and feeding on the remains with no grati=
tude and no attempt to replenish what it is that you=E2=80=99re taking from=
 it.=20
That=E2=80=99s one mythological motif.=20
<br><br>The warning in the flood story is, don=E2=80=99t do that for very l=
ong, because things will happen that are so awful that you cannot possibly =
imagine it. That=E2=80=99ll happen to you personally; it=E2=80=99ll happen =
to your family; it=E2=80=99ll happen to your community, and it=E2=80=99s ha=
ppened to people over and over throughout history. It=E2=80=99s quite inter=
esting. It=E2=80=99s very soon after the story of Cain and Abel when you se=
e evil enter the world. In the story of Adam and Eve, along with self-consc=
iousness, the evil, there, is the knowledge of good and evil; that=E2=80=99=
s the ability to self-consciously hurt other people. Of course, instantly, =
Cain takes that to the absolute extreme. He uses that capacity to destroy, =
really, what he loves best. He gets as close as a human being can to destro=
ying the divine ideal. Of course, his brother is Abel, and Abel is favoured=
 by God. Cain destroys him. Cain tells God at the end of that episode that =
his punishment is more than he can bear. I think the reason for that is, wh=
ere are you when you destroy your own ideal? What=E2=80=99s left for you? T=
here=E2=80=99s nowhere to go. There=E2=80=99s no up, and when there=E2=80=
=99s no up, there=E2=80=99s a lot of down.=20
<br><br>There=E2=80=99s an idea that was put forth very nicely in Milton=E2=
=80=99s <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Lost" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">=
Paradise Lost</span></a> when he was describing, from a psychological persp=
ective, essentially what hell is: you=E2=80=99re in hell to the degree that=
 you=E2=80=99re distant from the good. That might be a good way of thinking=
 about it. If you destroy your own ideal=E2=80=94which you do with jealousy=
, resentment, and the desire to pull down people that you would like to be=
=E2=80=94then you end up in a situation that=E2=80=99s indistinguishable fr=
om hell. The way the Biblical story unfolds is, well, it=E2=80=99s Cain, an=
d then it=E2=80=99s the flood. Cain adopts this mode of being that=E2=80=99=
s antithetical to being itself=E2=80=94at least to positive being itself. H=
e does it knowing full well what he=E2=80=99s doing. The net consequence of=
 that, as it ripples through the entire social structure, is that God stand=
s back and says, this whole thing has got so bad that the only thing we can=
 do is wipe it to the ground. That is no joke. That=E2=80=99s exactly how t=
hings work.=20
<br><br>One of the things that=E2=80=99s extraordinarily terrifying about t=
hat sequence of stories=E2=80=94and I believe this to be true. I think I re=
alized this independently of any of the analysis that I was doing of mythol=
ogical stories. I looked at what happened in places like the Soviet Union, =
Maoist China, and Nazi Germany. The most penetrating observers of those soc=
ieties, the people who were most interested in how it was that those absolu=
te catastrophes came about, all said the same thing: it was rooted in the d=
egeneration of the individuals who made up the society. You hear that peopl=
e were following orders. No; that explanation doesn=E2=80=99t hold water. Y=
ou hear that you would be punished if you resisted. There was some truth in=
 that, but nowhere near as much as people might think, especially at the be=
ginnings of the process. It was more that people decided=E2=80=94each and e=
very one of them=E2=80=94to turn a blind eye to the catastrophes and to par=
ticipate in the lies. That warped entire societies, and they veered their w=
ay downward to something as closely approximating hell as you could manage,=
 especially in places like Nazi Germany and, well, in all three of those pl=
aces.=20
<br><br>One of the things that=E2=80=99s so frightening about the stories i=
n Genesis is that they say something very clear: your moral degeneration co=
ntributes in no small way to the degeneration of the entire cosmos. You say=
, well, I would like my life to be meaningful. People say that. Really? Wou=
ld you really like your life to be meaningful? You=E2=80=99d think people w=
ould trade a little nihilism for not having to face that particular realiza=
tion. I think people do that all the time. It=E2=80=99s a terrible weight t=
o realize. But we are networked together. That=E2=80=99s the vulnerability =
that=E2=80=99s associated with our intense capacity to communicate. It=E2=
=80=99s certainly possible that the ripples of our individual actions have =
consequences that are far beyond the limits of our immediate consciousness.=
 I also think that people know that, too. They know that in the way that pe=
ople know things when they don=E2=80=99t want to know them, which means the=
y know them embodied; they can feel them; they can sense them; they have an=
 emotional response to them, but there=E2=80=99s no damn way they=E2=80=99r=
e going to let them become articulated, because they don=E2=80=99t want to =
know. When you=E2=80=99re feeling guilty and ashamed about the things you=
=E2=80=99ve done or not done=E2=80=94I know that can get out of hand, as we=
ll=E2=80=94it=E2=80=99s often because there is a crooked little part of you=
 that=E2=80=99s aiming at the worst possible outcome.=20
<br><br>One of the things Jung said about the <a href=3D"https://web.archiv=
e.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_(psychology)"=
 target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">shadow</span></a>=E2=80=94Jung=
=E2=80=99s famous idea that everyone has a dark side, and that that dark si=
de needs to be incorporated and made conscious=E2=80=94was that the shadow =
of the human being reaches all the way to hell. That=E2=80=99s the thing th=
at=E2=80=99s so interesting about reading Carl Jung: he actually means what=
 he says. It=E2=80=99s not a metaphor. The part of you that=E2=80=99s twist=
ed against being is aligned with the part of the conscious cosmos, let=E2=
=80=99s say, that=E2=80=99s aiming at making everything as terrible as it c=
an possibly be.=20
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s a terrible shock to realize that. That=E2=80=99s partl=
y why people don=E2=80=99t realize it. It=E2=80=99s something that people k=
eep at an arm=E2=80=99s length. It=E2=80=99s the same as recognizing yourse=
lf as a Nazi concentration camp guard, which is a very useful exercise. The=
re=E2=80=99s absolutely no reason why you couldn=E2=80=99t have been or sti=
ll could be one. And if you think otherwise, all the more reason for assumi=
ng that you would be unable to resist the temptation if it was, in fact, of=
fered to you. And if you don=E2=80=99t think it=E2=80=99s a temptation, the=
n there=E2=80=99s so much that you don=E2=80=99t know about human beings th=
at you=E2=80=99re not even in the game. If it wasn=E2=80=99t a temptation, =
then people bloody well wouldn=E2=80=99t have done it. Plenty of people did=
 it, and it=E2=80=99s no wonder.
<br><br>So things get serious in Genesis very, very rapidly. The depth of t=
he seriousness is ultimate=E2=80=94archetypal. It gets as serious as it can=
 get. The story of Noah and the flood opens in a fragmentary manner. I beli=
eve that these passages are a part of a longer story that we only have  bit=
s and pieces of, and also that it=E2=80=99s part of more than one story.<in=
