<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>"And God saw that the wickedness of man was g=
reat in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart =
was only evil continually."
<br><br>One of the things I really didn=E2=80=99t like about going to churc=
h when I was kid=E2=80=94I went to a pretty moderate church. It was the Uni=
ted Church, which is hardly even become a church now. It=E2=80=99s so moder=
ate, so to speak. One of the things I didn=E2=80=99t like was the constant =
harping by the ministry on the sinful nature of human beings. It didn=E2=80=
=99t speak to me properly, partly because I really didn=E2=80=99t understan=
d what it meant, and partly because it seemed sort of self-flagellating in =
an unattractive way. I don=E2=80=99t know if there is an attractive way to =
be self-flagellating. There was something about it that was also rote and f=
ake that I didn=E2=80=99t like. But, you know, I thought about that more in=
 later years. I started to understand that there was some real utility in a=
sking people to keep the evil that they=E2=80=99re doing clear and consciou=
s in the forefront of their imagination.=20
<br><br>I think I mentioned to you guys last week this little episode from =
what we know of Mesopotamian culture, surrounding the emperor and the New Y=
ear=E2=80=99s festival. They would take the emperor outside of the walled c=
ity and strip him of his garb, so that he was reduced to just an ordinary m=
an. And then they would humiliate him ritually and ask him how it was that,=
 over the last year, he wasn=E2=80=99t a spectacular embodiment of <a href=
=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Marduk" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Marduk</span></a>. Mar=
duk was the Mesopotamian deity who made order out of chaos, essentially. Th=
e emperor was supposed to sit and think, well, ok, I=E2=80=99m emperor. I s=
hould be doing a good job. Maybe I should even be doing a great job. But, p=
robably, I=E2=80=99m coming up short in a bunch of ways, and that actually =
happens to be important, as I=E2=80=99m running the entire show. I should b=
e very, very cognizant of how I=E2=80=99m failing to live up to the ideal.=
=20
<br><br>That is the constant clarion call=E2=80=94that=E2=80=99s degenerate=
, I would say=E2=80=94in institutional Christianity. That was actually the =
idea: look, theres a bunch of ways that you=E2=80=99re not being everything=
 you could be. It=E2=80=99s not supposed to be a whip, or to knock you down=
. Although, maybe it=E2=80=99s a whip to knock down your pride=E2=80=94prid=
e that stops you from being aware of your insufficiencies. It=E2=80=99s mor=
e like a call to the opposite. It=E2=80=99s like, well, you should stop doi=
ng those things. You could be so much more than you are. That would be so m=
uch better for you and everyone else that it=E2=80=99s just not good that y=
ou continue breaking your own rules, let=E2=80=99s say.
<br><br><a id=3D"C3"></a><div align=3D"middle"><a id=3D"C3"><span class=3D"=
"><font size=3D"4">Section III</font></span></a><br><font size=3D"1"><a hre=
f=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://youtu.be/6gFjB9FTN5=
8?t=3D2161" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">TIMESTAMP</span></a>=
</font></div>As I said, we could start this game by assuming that you shoul=
d at least play the game that you=E2=80=99re playing straight. And it is th=
e case that, if you watch yourself=E2=80=A6It=E2=80=99s a terrifying thing =
to do, but if you watch yourself, you=E2=80=99ll see that you lie a lot. Wh=
en I learned this, to begin with, I was in my 20s. I=E2=80=99m a smart pers=
on, and I was very proud of that. I was also a small person, and I was move=
d ahead one year in school, so I was a very small person in my classes. I w=
as also very mouthy=E2=80=94which might not come as much of a surprise=E2=
=80=94and somewhat provocative. I got pushed around a fair bit=E2=80=94beca=
use everyone gets pushed around=E2=80=94and my weapon was to be mouthy. It =
was a fairly effective weapon, although it tended to backfire. If you=E2=80=
=99re effectively mouthy with large, obnoxious people, then they tend to re=
spond in a relatively negative, physical way. That sort of thing was happen=
ing to me a fair bit. But I was quite proud of the fact that I had some int=
ellectual power.=20
<br><br>It was then, in my 20s, when I learned about some of the danger of =
that. I started to read Milton=E2=80=99s Paradise Lost. I started to unders=
tand the danger of the intellect. The danger of the intellect, as far as I =
can tell, is that it tends towards pride and arrogance. It also tends to fa=
ll in love with its own productions. In Paradise Lost, that=E2=80=99s Lucif=
er. Lucifer is the intellect that falls in love with its own productions, a=
nd then presumes that there=E2=80=99s nothing outside of what it thinks. Th=
at=E2=80=99s the totalitarian mentality: We have a total system, and we kno=
w how everything works. We are going to implement it, and that will bring a=
bout heaven on earth. That=E2=80=99s associated with intellectual arrogance=
.=20
<br><br>At the same time, another thing was happening to me. I was noticing=
 my intellectual arrogance, and I started to understand what that meant. I =
also started to understand that there was more to life than the intellect=
=E2=80=94much more. I smoked too much, and I drank too much, and I weighed =
like 130 pounds. I wasn=E2=80=99t in good physical shape. I had a lot of th=
ings to do, when I went to graduate school, to put myself together. At the =
same time, I was trying to understand why things had gone so crazily wrong =
with the world=E2=80=94its encapsulation in the Cold War, and what role I m=
ight be playing in that=E2=80=94if any=E2=80=94and what role any of us were=
 playing in that. At the same time, I was working at a prison, only a littl=
e bit. I worked with this crazy psychologist. He used to put jokes on his m=
ultiple choice tests. He was a really eccentric guy. I really liked his cou=
rses. He taught a course on creativity, and he was also a prison psychologi=
st. He was an eccentric guy. For some reason, he liked me=E2=80=94maybe bec=
ause I was eccentric, too. He invited me to go out to the Edmonton maximum =
security prison with him a couple of times, which I did. That was a very in=
teresting experience. I was trying to figure out what role each individual=
=E2=80=99s behaviour bore to the pathology of the group. It was something l=
ike that.=20
<br><br>I went out to the prison, and I met a little guy, smaller than me. =
I was a little bigger by then. He was a pretty innocuous guy. The prison lo=
oked like a high school=E2=80=94which is really quite telling, in my estima=
tion=E2=80=94and I was out in the gymnasium. There were all these monsters =
in there, weightlifting. I remember one guy, who was tattooed everywhere. H=
e had a huge scar running down the middle of his chest. It looked like some=
body had hit him with an axe. I was in there, and I had this weird cape tha=
t I used to wear, that I=E2=80=99d bought in Portugal, and some boots to go=
 along with it. Yeah=E2=80=A6It was like a 1890s Sherlock Holmes cape. It w=
as from the 1890s, because this little village was up on a hill. It was a w=
alled city on a hill, and they sold these things. I don=E2=80=99t think the=
y=E2=80=99d changed the style since 1890, so I though they were really cool=
. So I was wearing that, which wasn=E2=80=99t, perhaps, the most conservati=
ve garb to don if you=E2=80=99re going to go to a maximum security prison.
<br><br>Anyways, I was in the gymnasium, and the psychologist left. God onl=
y knows=E2=80=A6I mean, that=E2=80=99s what he was like. All these guys cam=
e around me, and they were offering to trade their prison clothes for my ca=
pe. I was being made an offer I couldn=E2=80=99t refuse. I didn=E2=80=99t r=
eally know what to do. And then this little guy said something like, the ps=
ychologist sent me to come and take you away, or something like that. And s=
o I thought, well, better this little guy than all these monsters.=20
<br><br>We went outside the gym, into the exercise yard. We were wandering =
around, and he was talking to me, and he seemed like a kind of innocuous gu=
y. And then the psychologist showed up at the door and motioned us back, wh=
ich was kind of a relief. I went into his office. He said, you know that gu=
y that you walked out in the yard with? I said, yeah. He said, one night he=
 took two cops and had them kneel down. While they were begging for their l=
ives, he shot them both in the back of the head. I thought, hmph=E2=80=A6
<br><br>See, the thing that was so interesting was that he was so innocuous=
, right? What you=E2=80=99d hope is that someone like that would be very mu=
ch unlike you, let=E2=80=99s say, and certainly wouldn=E2=80=99t be like so=
meone innocuous that you=E2=80=99d met. What you=E2=80=99d want is that the=
 guy would be like half werewolf and half vampire, so you could just tell r=
ight away that he was a coldblooded killer. But no. He was this sort of ine=
ffectual, little guy, who was certainly not ineffectual if you gave him a r=
evolver and the upper hand.
<br><br>That made me think a lot about the relationship between being innoc=
uous and being dangerous. Another thing happened=E2=80=94I met another guy =
out there. A week or two later, I heard that he and a friend of his had hel=
d another guy down and pulverized his left leg with a lead pipe. The reason=
 for that was that they thought that he was a snitch, and maybe he was. Tha=
t time, I did something different. Instead of being shocked and horrified b=
y that=E2=80=94although I certainly was=E2=80=94I thought, how in the world=
 could you do that? Because I didn=E2=80=99t think I could do that. I thoug=
ht that there as a qualitative distinction between me and those people. I s=
pent about two weeks trying to see if I could figure out under what conditi=
ons I could do that=E2=80=94what kind of psychological transformation I wou=
ld have to undergo to be able to do that. That was a meditative exercise, l=
et=E2=80=99s say. It only took about 10 days for me to realize that not onl=
y could I do that, but that it would be a hell of a lot easier than I had t=
hought it would be. That=E2=80=99s sort of where that wall between me and w=
hat Jung described as the shadow started to fall apart. That, also, was ver=
y useful; I started to treat myself as a somewhat different entity.=20
<br><br>I thought I was a good guy, and there=E2=80=99s no reason for me to=
 think that. You=E2=80=99re not a good guy unless you really made a bloody =
effort to be a good guy. You=E2=80=99re just not. It=E2=80=99s not easy. An=
d so you=E2=80=99re probably a moderately bad guy. That=E2=80=99s a long wa=
ys from being an absolutely horrible guy, but it=E2=80=99s also a long ways=
 from being a good guy. But I had a little more respect for myself after th=
at, because I also understood that there was a monstrous element to the hum=
an psyche that you needed to respect, and that was part of you. And I under=
stood that you should regard yourself, in some sense, as a loaded weapon. I=
t=E2=80=99s very useful to regard yourself as a loaded weapon around childr=
en, because, around children, you are a loaded weapon. The terrible experie=
nces that many children have with their parents are testament to that.=20
<br><br>Anyways, at about the same time=E2=80=94and I don=E2=80=99t exactly=
 know how these things were causally related. I guess it was because I was =
trying to figure out who I was and how that could be fixed. Something like =
that. I started to pay very careful attention to what I was saying. I don=
=E2=80=99t know if that happened voluntarily or involuntarily, but I could =
feel a sort of split developing in my psyche. I=E2=80=99ve actually had stu=
dents tell me that the same thing has happened to them after they=E2=80=99v=
e listened to some of the material that I=E2=80=99ve been describing to all=
 of you. But I split into two, let=E2=80=99s say.=20
<br><br>One part was the old me that was talking a lot, that liked to argue=
, and that liked ideas. There was another part that was watching that part,=
 just with its eyes opened, and neutrally judging. The part that was neutra=
lly judging was watching the part that was talking, and going, that wasn=E2=
=80=99t your idea; you don=E2=80=99t really believe that; you don=E2=80=99t=
 really know what you=E2=80=99re talking about; that isn=E2=80=99t true. I =
thought, hm! That=E2=80=99s really interesting! That was happening to like =
95 percent of what I was saying, and then I didn=E2=80=99t really know what=
 to do. I thought, ok, this is strange. Maybe I fragmented, and that=E2=80=
=99s just not a good thing, at all. It=E2=80=99s not like I was hearing voi=
ces, or anything like that. It wasn=E2=80=99t like that. People have multip=
le parts.
<br><br>So then I had this weird conundrum: which of these two things are m=
e? Is it the part that=E2=80=99s listening and saying, no, that=E2=80=99s r=
ubbish; that=E2=80=99s a lie; you=E2=80=99re doing that to impress people; =
you=E2=80=99re just trying to win the argument. Was that me? Or was I the p=
art that was going about its normal, verbal business? I didn=E2=80=99t know=
, but I decided that I would go with the critic. And then what I tried to d=
o=E2=80=94what I learned to do, I think=E2=80=94was to stop saying things t=
hat made me weak. I mean, I=E2=80=99m still trying to do that. I=E2=80=99m =
always feeling, when I talk, whether or not the words that I am saying are =
making me align or making me come apart. I really do think that the alignme=
nt is the right way to conceptualize it, because if you say things as true =
as you can say them, then they come out of the depths inside of you. We don=
=E2=80=99t know where thoughts come from. We don=E2=80=99t know how far dow=
n into your substructure the thoughts emerge. We don=E2=80=99t know what pr=
ocess of physiological alignment is necessary for you to speak from the cor=
e of your being. We don=E2=80=99t understand any of that=E2=80=94we don=E2=
=80=99t even conceptualize that. But I believe that you can feel that.=20
<br><br>I learned some of that by reading <a href=3D"https://web.archive.or=
g/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Rogers" target=3D"_=
blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Carl Rogers</span></a>, who=E2=80=99s a gre=
at clinician. He talked about mental health, in part, as the coherence betw=
een the spiritual=E2=80=94or the abstract=E2=80=94and the physical=E2=80=94=
that the two things were aligned. There=E2=80=99s a lot of ideas of alignme=
nt in psychoanalytic and clinical thinking. But, anyways, I decided that I =
would start practicing not saying things that would make me weak. What happ=
ened was that I had to stop saying almost everything that I was saying=E2=
=80=9495 percent of it. That=E2=80=99s a hell of a shock=E2=80=94this was o=
ver a few months=E2=80=94to wake up and realize that you=E2=80=99re mostly =
deadwood. It=E2=80=99s a shock. You might think, well, do you really want a=
ll of that to burn off? Well, there=E2=80=99s nothing left but a little hus=
k=E2=80=945 percent of you. Well, if that 5 percent is solid, then maybe th=
at=E2=80=99s exactly what you want to have happen.<input class=3D"spoilerbu=
tton" type=3D"button" value=3D"OPEN SLIDE"><div class=3D"spoiler">
