<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>"But Noah found grace in the eyes of the Lord=
. These are the generations of Noah: Noah was a just man and perfect in his=
 generations, And Noah walked with God."
<br><br>That=E2=80=99s an interesting line. If you remember back in the sto=
ry of Adam and Eve, what happened to Adam=E2=80=94once he ate of the fruit =
of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, woke up, the scales fell fro=
m his eyes, he became self-conscious, and he developed the knowledge of goo=
d and evil=E2=80=94is that he won=E2=80=99t walk with God when God calls hi=
m in the garden. And so Noah is Adam without the fall, essentially. There=
=E2=80=99s something that Noah=E2=80=99s doing right, that motivates God to=
 spare him=E2=80=94or maybe to show him a pathway through the emerging chao=
s. Something like that. That=E2=80=99s worth thinking about, a lot.=20
<br><br>There will be situations in your life where what you face is the em=
ergent chaos. Maybe that will be some terrible catastrophe inside your fami=
ly, or maybe it will be something that=E2=80=99s occurring on a much broade=
r social level, but chaos is coming. Unless you want to be a denizen of the=
 chaos, or even a contributor to it=E2=80=94and perhaps that is what you wa=
nt; many people under those circumstances choose that=E2=80=94what you=E2=
=80=99re going to want to know is how to build an ark and get through it. I=
f you=E2=80=99re interested in life, and if you=E2=80=99re interested in pr=
oper being, and if you=E2=80=99re disinclined to produce any more suffering=
 than necessary, then you want to know how to conduct yourself when the cat=
astrophe comes, so that you have a reasonable possibility of moving through=
 it and starting anew.=20
<br><br>When this old story says, well, God=E2=80=99s not happy, and he=E2=
=80=99s going to wipe everything out, it=E2=80=99s like, you might want to =
take that seriously. And then when it says, but there was one person who ha=
d a mode of being that protected him from that, that=E2=80=99s also somethi=
ng that you might want to take seriously. You might want to know what that =
being is=E2=80=94you might need to use it. These sorts of things are practi=
cal in the deepest possible sense. They=E2=80=99re real in the deepest poss=
ible sense, and practical in the deepest practical sense. So Noah walked wi=
th God.
<br><br><a id=3D"C4"></a><div align=3D"middle"><a id=3D"C4"><span class=3D"=
"><font size=3D"4">Section IV</font></span></a><br><font size=3D"1"><a href=
=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://youtu.be/6gFjB9FTN58=
?t=3D3414" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">TIMESTAMP</span></a><=
/font></div>I=E2=80=99m going to switch way ahead, here. I said at the begi=
nning of the lecture series that the Bible is a hyperlinked text, and every=
thing refers to everything else. There=E2=80=99s utility in reading it in l=
inear order, but it=E2=80=99s not a linear document. There=E2=80=99s an inf=
inite number of pathways that you can use to walk through it. All of the do=
cument expands upon and refers to all of the rest of the document. And so I=
