<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>"And why beholdest thou the mote that is in t=
hy brother=E2=80=99s eye, but considerest not the beam that is in thine own=
 eye? Or how wilt thou say to thy brother, Let me pull out the mote out of =
thine eye; and, behold, a beam is in thine own eye?"
<br><br>You might be wondering what a beam is. A mote is a dust speck, and =
a beam is a very large piece of lumber. And so the issue is not so much the=
 blindness of others=E2=80=94even though there=E2=80=99s as much blindness =
among others as there is for you. The issue, here=E2=80=94the description, =
here=E2=80=94is that you should be concerned about what=E2=80=99s interferi=
ng with your own vision, first. You should leave other people the hell alon=
e in relationship to that. And so, if your mode of being in the world is su=
ch that you want others to act better to improve things for you=E2=80=94or =
if you identify the evil and the catastrophe as something that=E2=80=99s ou=
tside, that someone else needs to fix, or that someone else is responsible =
for=E2=80=94then you=E2=80=99re not going to fix that. You=E2=80=99re going=
 to remain blind to the things that you=E2=80=99re doing and not doing that=
 make things not go well. And so it=E2=80=99s just better to think, right, =
I=E2=80=99m probably blind in many, many ways. Maybe there are some ways th=
at I can rectify that.=20
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s highly probable that you=E2=80=99re blind in all sorts=
 of ways. In fact, it=E2=80=99s virtually certain, and so it=E2=80=99s just=
 more useful to think, how is it that I=E2=80=99m wrong in this situation? =
I=E2=80=99ll tell you something that I learned to do when I was already wit=
h my wife, which happened frequently. When you actually communicate with pe=
ople, you find out that there are many things you don=E2=80=99t agree on. T=
hat=E2=80=99s because you=E2=80=99re actually different creatures. If you=
=E2=80=99re actually going to have a truthful conversation, then you=E2=80=
=99re going to find out that you don=E2=80=99t see things the same way. The=
n you can either pretend that=E2=80=99s not the case and gloss over it, and=
 then end up in a 30-year, silent war, or you can have the damn fight when =
you need to have it and see if you can straighten it out.=20
<br><br>Now and then we would get in a situation when we were at <a href=3D=
"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/=
at_loggerheads#English" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">loggerhe=
ads</span></a>; we couldn=E2=80=99t move. It would spiral up into hate spee=
ch, let=E2=80=99s say. Yeah. Everyone laughs, because they know they manife=
st plenty of hate speech towards those they love. So one of the things we l=
earned to do was, when we hit an impasse, was to separate and go our own wa=
ys, and sit, and think, ok, we=E2=80=99re at this unpleasant situation. We =
can=E2=80=99t figure out how to move forward. I=E2=80=99d always think, of =
course it=E2=80=99s her fault. Obviously it=E2=80=99s her fault=E2=80=94at =
least 95 percent. But maybe there was something I did that contributed like=
 5 percent to it. I would sit and think, and ask myself a question: Is ther=
e anything I did in the last 6 months that increased the probability that t=
his impasse would manifest itself? I=E2=80=99ll tell you, you have no idea =
how fast your mind will generate an answer to a question like that. There=
=E2=80=99s undoubtedly some idiotic thing that you did, that you know, that=
 you remember, that increased the probability that you=E2=80=99re going to =
have your hands around the throat of the person that you love. And then you=
 can go tell them that. And then you can have a conversation=E2=80=94especi=
ally if they do the same thing. You say, look, here=E2=80=99s how I=E2=80=
=99m an idiot in this situation. The other person says, well, yeah. Here=E2=
