/font></div>Jung thought about this. He thought that people had two poles o=
f consciousness. One was the individual consciousness that we each identify=
 with. The other was something he called the <a href=3D"https://web.archive=
.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self_in_Jungian_psych=
ology" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Self</span></a>. You migh=
t think of the Self as the divine within. That=E2=80=99s a close enough app=
roximation. It=E2=80=99s the universal part of your consciousness=E2=80=94i=
t=E2=80=99s your conscience. That=E2=80=99s another way of thinking about i=
t, whatever your conscience is. But it=E2=80=99s something that you can con=
sult.=20
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s like the Socratic <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/w=
eb/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daemon_(classical_mythology=
)" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">daemon</span></a>. Socrates s=
aid that the thing that made him different from everyone else in Greece was=
 that he consulted his daemon, his genius. He asked himself how it was that=
 he should conduct himself in the world, and then he did that, whatever it =
was. He didn=E2=80=99t try to force a solution. He didn=E2=80=99t try to fo=
rce a solution selfishly. He asked: I=E2=80=99m going to manifest myself in=
 the best possible manner in the world. I would like to do that. What would=
 that be? Well, you=E2=80=99re perfectly capable of thinking=E2=80=94God on=
ly knows how. You=E2=80=99re perfectly capable of immense feats of imaginat=
ion, dream, and fantasy. God only knows how you do all of that. What would =
happen if you consulted yourself about the best possible outcome for you? Y=
ou might get an answer. Well, that=E2=80=99s what this proposition is.<inpu=
