<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>"Therefore all things whatsoever ye would tha=
t men should do to you, do ye even so to them: for this is the law and the =
prophets."
<br><br>That=E2=80=99s a reciprocity issue, right? This is another thing I =
learned from Jung. Jung reversed this. This is the <a href=3D"https://web.a=
rchive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Rule" ta=
rget=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Golden Rule</span></a>, and it=E2=
=80=99s often read as, be nice to other people. It=E2=80=99s like, that is =
not what this rule means. It doesn=E2=80=99t mean that even a little bit. I=
t means something like=E2=80=94and we=E2=80=99ll reverse it so that it conc=
entrates on you rather than on the other person, to begin with. It means so=
mething like, conceptualize how things could be great=E2=80=94if they were =
great for you, if you were taking care of yourself=E2=80=94and then work to=
 make that the case for everyone else.=20
<br><br>You see that in Buddhism. Buddha reached nirvana. That=E2=80=99s th=
e theory. He was tempted with the offer to stay there. He rejected that off=
er, and came back to the profane world. He felt that the attainment of nirv=
ana was insufficient unless everyone attained it simultaneously. It=E2=80=
=99s something like that. It=E2=80=99s to treat yourself properly. That=E2=
=80=99s a hard thing to do, because you=E2=80=99re a fallen, shameful, cowa=
rdly, deceitful, malevolent, mortal creature. And so it=E2=80=99s not easy,=
 and you know it. It=E2=80=99s not easy to treat something like that proper=
ly.=20
<br><br>It isn=E2=80=99t obvious that people treat themselves better than t=
hey treat other people. I don=E2=80=99t think that=E2=80=99s obvious, at al=
l. But, maybe, you could start with yourself, and think, ok, I=E2=80=99m go=
ing to take care of myself as if I have value. What would that look like? A=
nd then I=E2=80=99m going to work to extend that courtesy to everyone else.=
 The hypothesis here is that, if you take all of the moral wisdom that mank=
ind has generated over its millennia of struggle, evolved and then manifest=
ed in metaphors and stories, and then codified into articulated law, and yo=
u pick one principle that dominated all of that, this would be the principl=
e.
<br><br>It=E2=80=99s interesting, too, because it=E2=80=99s "the law and th=
e prophets." The law is the rule, but the prophets are the process by which=
 the rules are being updated. And so the prophets are superordinate, in som=
e sense, to the law. The proposition that=E2=80=99s set forth in this parti=
cular statement is that this maxim, which is to optimize your own mode of b=
eing and then to work to do the same for everyone around you, is not only t=
he thing that=E2=80=99s at the core of the law, but it=E2=80=99s at the cor=
e of the process that generates and updates the law. It=E2=80=99s a hell of=
 a thing for someone to say.<input class=3D"spoilerbutton" type=3D"button" =
value=3D"OPEN SLIDE"><div class=3D"spoiler">
