<div class=3D"clearfix"></div>"Many will say to me in that day"=E2=80=94tha=
t=E2=80=99s judgement day=E2=80=94"Lord, have we not prophesied in thy name=
? and in thy name have cast out devils? and in thy name done many wonderful=
 works? And then will I profess unto them, I never knew you: depart from me=
, ye that work iniquity."=20
<br><br>That=E2=80=99s judgement day. That=E2=80=99s an archetypal idea. Pa=
rtly it=E2=80=99s archetypal because every day is judgement day. The part o=
f you that=E2=80=99s equivalent to the logos, the part of you that=E2=80=99=
s your own ideal, sits in eternal judgement on your iniquity. That=E2=80=99=
s the source of guilt, shame, withdrawal, and then resentment, murderousnes=
s, and genocide. It=E2=80=99s because you can intuit the ideal. The problem=
 with intuiting the ideal is that an ideal is always a judge. There is no d=
ifference between an ideal and a judge, and so you=E2=80=99re eternally jud=
ged by your own ideal. If you have no ideal, then you=E2=80=99ve got no dir=
ection and no meaning in your life. And, of course, the more extreme the id=
eal, the harsher the judge.=20
<br><br>Jung was very curious about why the <a href=3D"https://web.archive.=
org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Revelation" ta=
rget=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Book of Revelation</span></a> was =
tacked onto the Bible, because the Book of Revelation is a very weird book.=
 In the gospels, Christ is, I would say, perhaps, primarily merciful. There=
=E2=80=99s a war in his character between truth and mercy, but it=E2=80=99s=
 one of the two=E2=80=94perhaps mercy. Jung=E2=80=99s observation was that =
the gospel Christ was too merciful, and that=E2=80=99s why the Book of Reve=
lation was tacked onto the New Testament. In the Book of Revelation, Christ=
, who=E2=80=99s the transcendent ideal and above the pyramid, is nothing bu=
t a judge, and everyone fails. Of course, the ultimate ideal is the ultimat=
e judge. That=E2=80=99s the archetypal reality, there. You can say, well, I=
 don=E2=80=99t want to be judged, and so I=E2=80=99ll dispense with the ide=
al. But then you=E2=80=99re Cain. Cain is exactly the person who dispenses =
with the ideal. There=E2=80=99s no escaping from it. There=E2=80=99s no esc=
aping from eternal judgement. That=E2=80=99s the archetypal story.<input cl=
