in is the breath of life, from under heaven; and every thing that is in the=
 earth shall die. But with thee will I establish my covenant; and thou shal=
t come into the ark, thou, and thy sons, and thy wife, and thy sons=E2=80=
=99 wives with thee."=20
<br><br>That=E2=80=99s a fairly optimistic twist on the story: not only is =
it Noah, but he gets to save his whole family, and down a couple of generat=
ions. That=E2=80=99s a good thing to think about.
<br><br>I had this client, and she had a very hard upbringing=E2=80=94not a=
 lot of encouragement, to say the least. Let=E2=80=99s say, a lot of discou=
ragement. She had a son, and what was really interesting about her in relat=
ionship to her son was that she refused to do to her son all the things tha=
t she could have learned to do to him=E2=80=94given her extensive experienc=
e with being made as miserable as possible by someone who was hellbent on b=
ringing her to her knees. She learned the opposite lesson, from all her mis=
ery and torment, which was not to move that forward down the generations. S=
o the idea, here, is that, if you walk properly, aim properly, act properly=
, and act with God in the manner that we=E2=80=99ve been discussing, perhap=
s that isn=E2=80=99t only for you. Perhaps it=E2=80=99s also the thing that=
 will save your family. And then, by implication, perhaps it will also save=
 society.
<br><br>That=E2=80=99s exactly what happens with Noah. First it=E2=80=99s h=
im, and then it=E2=80=99s his family, but everything else goes. By acting p=
roperly and saving himself and his family, he actually saves the world. The=
 most profound people that I=E2=80=99ve read, who=E2=80=99ve meditated deep=
ly on the problem of, say, totalitarian catastrophe=E2=80=94and I would put=
 <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Solzhenitsyn" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText=
">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</span></a> at the top of that list. His entire cor=
pus=E2=80=94three volumes, 700 pages long, each in tiny type=E2=80=94is a l=
ong scream about the absolute necessity of individual honesty and ethical b=
ehaviour as the only bulwark against totalitarian catastrophe. I=E2=80=99ve=
 read many writers who=E2=80=99ve attempted to diagnose the problems of the=
 20th century. I think Solzhenitsyn came to the same conclusions that <a hr=
ef=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20181221012830/https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Viktor_Frankl" target=3D"_blank"><span class=3D"blueText">Viktor Frankl=
</span></a> came to as a consequence of his experiences in the Nazi concent=
ration camps.=20
<br><br>I=E2=80=99m also an admirer of Frankl, but Solzhenitsyn takes it to=
 an entirely different level of profundity and makes an extraordinarily str=
ong case that not only do societies deteriorate because the people within t=
he societies become individually corrupt, but that the only way to stave th=
at off is for the individuals within that society to reject that corruption=
, in the confines of their own personal lives. He tells endless stories of =
people that he met in the Gulag=E2=80=94the work camps, the death camps, in=
 the Soviet Union=E2=80=94who were so incredibly tough that even under the =
most possible extreme conditions there wasn=E2=80=99t a chance that they we=
re going to step off that straight and narrow line. There was nothing the a=
uthorities could do to move them. Just watching that was enough to transfor=
m Solzhenitsyn. Of course, one of the things he wondered was=E2=80=94after =
spending a good amount of time in the work camps=E2=80=94well, just exactly=
 how did I get here? And it wasn=E2=80=99t, well, it was Hitler=E2=80=99s f=
ault, and it was Stalin=E2=80=99s fault=E2=80=94although, it was definitely=
 the fault of both of them. For Solzhenitsyn, it was also his fault, becaus=
e he=E2=80=99s playing the same game. He just wasn=E2=80=99t as good at it.=
